In 1992, Dynamix and Sierra On-Line released *Johnny Castaway*. Your Windows 3.1 PC went idle and Johnny fished, slept, built rafts, and got into trouble on his island. As a young kid, I came across him during a summer holiday trip to my parents' office.

Thirty-four years later, he is hard to visit. Sharing it with my kid means dragging a beige tower out of a closet or faffing about with a virtual machine. That is enough friction that almost nobody bothers, and a far cry from the little moment of joy when Johnny just did his thing on the side.

[Now he spends his days in my browser tab](https://khawkins98.github.io/johnny_web/).

Rebuilding his island paradise faithfully in JavaScript was only feasible with AI assistance; by hand, it would have been weeks of detailed full-time work. Earlier ports got a long way, and this one builds on them, but as far as I can tell none had been checked tick by tick against the original program (if I've missed one, please tell me).

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Johnny's island combines scripted gags and weighted random choices with an eleven-day story that advances at most one step per calendar day.
> - The browser port builds on earlier reverse-engineering work and interprets the original game resources in JavaScript.
> - Running the original Windows program under instrumentation provides reference traces, which is what kept AI-suggested interpretations honest. Separate rendering “goldens” catch changes in the port.

## Inside a gag, and between gags

The original files contain artwork, sound, and small programs describing the animations. They use the [Dynamix Game Development System (DGDS)](https://github.com/scummvm/scummvm/tree/master/engines/dgds), the engine Dynamix built for its adventure games. My browser implementation, [Bottle DGDS](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#system-overview), parses those resources and runs their bytecode instructions in JavaScript.

The scripts operate on two levels:

- **[ADS (Animation Director Script)](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#resource-and-script-model) files coordinate a gag.** They act as the high-level sequencer: starting and stopping scenes, evaluating random branches, and waiting for other actions to finish.
- **[TTM (Tiny Templated Movie)](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#resource-and-script-model) files perform the animation.** They handle per-frame work: loading bitmap sprites, drawing sprite frames, waiting on timer ticks, playing sound samples, and looping.

The [“Eight Arm Menace” scene flow](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/scene-flows/FISHING.ADS.md#eight-arm-menace-fishingads-tag-3) shows how they fit together: Johnny gets his pole, casts, reels in a heavy catch, and reaches the octopus gag. Several animation threads run concurrently. Their relative order and timing determine whether the result lands as a coherent joke.

Deciding which gag comes next is a separate problem. The original Windows executable wrapped a scene director around these scripts. It picked an eligible story scene, queued up everyday filler scenes before it, and scheduled Johnny's walks between their starting and ending positions. The browser port rebuilds that [scene-selection behaviour](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/johnny-host-behavior.md) outside the script interpreter.

That separation explained why earlier web ports felt incomplete. They could parse and play individual animations, but without the director, ordinary playback never selected them. Decoding a file tells you how to render an animation, but not when Johnny should decide to walk across the beach.

## Asking the original what it actually does

I didn't start from scratch. [Alexandre Fontoura's Castaway](https://github.com/xesf/castaway) provided the working JavaScript foundation for decoding resources. Jérémie Guillaume's [Johnny Reborn](https://github.com/jno6809/jc_reborn), a C port that already had a working scene scheduler, gave me the first scene catalogue. Hans Milling's [C# remake](https://github.com/nivs1978/Johnny-Castaway-Open-Source) and ScummVM's [DGDS engine](https://github.com/scummvm/scummvm/tree/master/engines/dgds), from [Vasco Costa](https://github.com/vcosta/scummvm/tree/master/engines/dgds) and the ScummVM team, supplied further findings and behavioural references. This project owes a great deal to their work.

Still, reverse engineering has an easy trap: an interpretation can look convincing on screen while being wrong underneath. The useful reference is the original 1992 program, running.

To get that, I built [capture tooling](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/tools/faithfulness-oracle/METHODOLOGY.md) around Windows 3.1 in a patched DOSBox-X emulator. The harness hooks the 16-bit executable's main script tick function. Each time it runs, roughly every 55 milliseconds on Windows 3.1's timer, the harness records which script threads are live (the “actors”) and their runstates; separate logs capture delay counters and random-number draws. Each capture is then condensed into a `timeline.jsonl` file. The emulator runs the same game data as the browser port, so a difference can't come from comparing two releases.

