Comparing threads rather than pixels was a lesson learned. My first tool compared captured screen frames. It found one real bug, a beach background vanishing behind a stale clock graphic, then lasted less than a day because it was slow and thrown off by palette and capture timing.

Checking the port against the original now comes down to four steps:

1. Run a gag in the instrumented emulator several times, so its random branches show up in the captures.
2. Distill those logs into reference fingerprints: JSON summaries of which script threads run together, their peak count, and their lifespans.
3. Run the browser engine and compare its tick-by-tick timeline against those fingerprints.
4. Investigate differences, with visual checks where the traces can't settle the question.

This is how the AI assistance worked in practice. LLMs are good at suggesting what an obscure assembly routine or opcode table might be doing, and just as good at sounding right when they're not. Running the original executable gave those suggestions something to answer to.

Opcode `0x2020` shows both sides. A thought bubble in “John's 1st Message” flashed past in a fraction of a second. Working from the decompiled code, an AI agent proposed a rule for the opcode with high confidence, and two fixes built on it were stopped when the traces flagged regressions. Instrumenting the emulator then turned up a handler the decompiler had missed, which sets a random hold on the frame. That was the fix. An AI hypothesis either matched the emulator trace, or it was wrong.

Other bugs were inherited and looked fine on screen. The code I forked picked random branches with equal odds, so Johnny left a fishing sequence about three times too often; the original weights each outcome. Both versions reached a plausible ending, but the port was taking the wrong path far too frequently. Cross-checking against the decompiled executable caught that one, before the traces existed.

Animation timing had similar traps. How long an animation is allowed to run is separate from how long each frame should pause, and mixing up those clocks changed how actions overlapped even when the drawings were correct. The traces also caught an opcode the inherited code treated as a no-op, which in the original ends the sequence. Ignoring it let a “preen” timer in one gag play four dead frames and run about five times too long. For weeks before that, a filter had papered over the symptoms in nineteen gags, on the theory that the reference captures had started mid-scene. Once the opcode was fixed, the filter had nothing left to do.

A second safety net catches regressions: [rendering goldens](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/test/render-goldens.mjs). These replay fragile sequences with fixed inputs and save compact fingerprints of drawing operations and pixels. A later code change that alters the output fails the check, and the difference gets reviewed. They need the original game data, so they run locally rather than in CI.

The two checks do different jobs. The traces establish what the port should do; the goldens preserve what it does now, including any mistake that made it into the baseline. Neither proves that every pixel, sound, or random path is correct. Even the random numbers match only up to a point: the port can reproduce the original generator and its weighted-choice rule (an opt-in setting; normal playback uses the browser's own random numbers), but not the exact run of choices a live session makes, because that depends on timing. The [coverage report](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/oracle-coverage.md) records what has been verified and where gaps remain.

## The machinery behind a living screensaver

With the scripts running faithfully, you can see how carefully Dynamix built the thing. Jeff Tunnell, who co-founded Dynamix and helped create Johnny Castaway, [summarized the core idea](https://jefftunnell.com/johnny-castaway-common-questions/):

> “So it generally runs a little loop that is interrupted by random events.”

The variety operates across multiple interlocking clocks. Within a day, the director chooses among eligible everyday scenes and gags using weighted probabilities. Across days, it follows an [eleven-day story](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/story-over-time.md), and its milestones advance as calendar days pass. The calendar was in the plan from the start. As Tunnell puts it: “That was my idea from the beginning. I wanted day and night, holidays, etc.”

