Reaching a day's key scene unlocks the next chapter. When your calendar date changes, the saved state advances one step toward that unlocked day. It avoids skipping ahead if you have been away for weeks, and day eleven wraps back to day one. The browser port keeps this progress in local storage.

Environmental details run on their own schedules. The tide moves through sixteen half-hour phases, an eight-hour cycle with about two hours at low tide; holiday decorations appear on specific calendar dates. The raft's construction progress, the island's position, and day-night lighting shifts all belong to the director's scheduler.

That structure is what kept me digging. Johnny's island is an authored story with random permutations running through it, rather than a stream of sight gags. Tunnell says it “was always pitched as the world's first story telling screen saver.” It is why the island felt alive, and it is a design that still isn't widely understood.

## The people behind the island

Tunnell's answers come from a [2022 Q&A on his blog](https://jefftunnell.com/johnny-castaway-common-questions/), written after a tweet about the screensaver drew a pile of questions from fans and a gaming historian. It is the best first-hand account of how Johnny came to be, and worth reading in full.

The idea is older than the screensaver. Over beers after work, Tunnell claimed he could make a game out of anything, even the bus stop he was looking at. Someone suggested a little guy stranded on an island instead. He carried that around for years, and when the flying toasters screensaver came along, he “just knew it had to be a screen saver.”

Johnny was one of the first three products from Jeff Tunnell Productions, the small group he split off from Dynamix's main office, built alongside *The Incredible Machine*. Rather than pitch it, Tunnell built a demo of Johnny walking around the island, climbing the palm trees, and fishing. It was green-lit in January and shipped in November.

The team was small:

- **Rich Rayl** worked out how to make a screensaver at all, at a time when Windows barely supported them, and wrote the final code.
- **Shawn Bird** designed the character.
- **Brian Hahn** came up with most of the gags, with Tunnell.
- **Sherry Wheeler**, an ex-Disney animator, drew the animation as pencil frames, which the team scanned and cleaned up for the low-resolution screens of the day.

> “She would bring in a pile of animations every week, and we would put them in the screen saver and then laugh at the result!”

Those cleaned-up frames are the sprites the TTM scripts still load and draw today. Asked how many scenes there are, Tunnell didn't remember, but recalled that “there are sites out there that used to have all of the sequences named and numbered.” The port now generates [an outline for every gag](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/scene-flows/README.md) from the scripts themselves. And he never expected fans to keep Johnny running on new machines: “The only surprise was how long it lasted.”

[Sierra Chest](https://sierrachest.com/index.php?a=games&id=255&title=johnny-castaway&fld=general) keeps the credits and release details, and the Internet Archive can [run the original 1992 version in DOSBox](https://archive.org/details/johnny-castaway-screensaver) if you want to compare. Nostalgia Nerd's retrospective covers the original release and why it caught on with so many of us in the 1990s:

