## Back on the island

The [browser port](https://khawkins98.github.io/johnny_web/) offers Classic and Enhanced playback. Classic keeps the original 640×480 scale, static clouds, and the original rolling waves; Enhanced adds responsive scaling, drifting clouds, and a small status display. The original Dynamix and Sierra pixel art, audio cues, and gags remain the center of the experience.

To run it, you supply the original game data. The [README](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web#readme) links the floppy archive, preserved on the Internet Archive by [Greyfalken](https://archive.org/details/@greyfalken); dropping its ZIP or extracted `.ima` disk image directly onto the page imports the resources straight into browser storage.

You don't have to wait for the calendar to see everything. Settings (press `S`) controls the display and can run time of day from the original sequence clock or your local clock. The developer panel (press `D`) previews holiday themes without touching your system clock, sets or advances the story day, and replays individual scenes. That said, the calendar is worth trusting at least once: visit between October 29 and 31 and the island is decorated for Halloween.

Next, I want to make the runtime leaner and build a way to map Johnny's eleven-day story as it unfolds. Like my [recent Mac OS 9 work](/posts/20260810-newsheep-sheepshaver-mac-os-9-2-apple-silicon-jit/), this started as an excuse to spend time with vintage software again, and the island held much more engineering than I expected from a screensaver.

